## Fastest way: the AutoSkill CLI

{# Only the line that installs *this* skill. Setting AutoSkill up on the machine — the wheel, the
   login, the assistant skill — used to be printed here too, on a page reached by someone who has
   already done all of it, since the page is inside the application they are logged into. Three
   commands where one was needed read as three things to do, and the one that mattered was the last. #}
```bash
{% if ctx.manifest_url %}
autoskill {{ "trial install" if ctx.trial else "install" }} --from {{ ctx.manifest_url }} --target {{ adapter.id }}{% if ctx.trial %} --token {{ ctx.trial_token or '<trial-token>' }}{% endif +%}
{% else %}
autoskill {{ "trial install" if ctx.trial else "install" }} --version-id {{ ctx.version_id }} --target {{ adapter.id }}{% if ctx.trial %} --token {{ ctx.trial_token or '<trial-token>' }}{% endif +%}
{% endif %}
```

No `autoskill` command yet on this machine? Set it up once from **My devices**
({{ ctx.server_url }}/me/devices), then come back here.

The CLI downloads {{ "everything the skill needs" if ctx.concise else "everything listed below" }}, verifies the
checksums, installs the shared components and the MCP servers, copies the skill into the right folder, registers
the MCP servers in `{{ adapter.mcp_config_path }}`, asks you for the required environment values and stores them
only in your agent's configuration.
